As promised after our meeting, here's an overview of Normain's role/permission model and how SharePoint access works — so you can plan your access provisioning.

**How the roles work:** each user has one **organization-level** role — either **Org owner** or **Org manager** — and, for every team they belong to, one **team-level** role — either **Team owner** or **Team member**. The four role columns below are therefore two either/or choices: one per org, one per team.

**Full permissions table:** attached *(Kalle to attach the catalog file)*.

**SharePoint-relevant permissions** — the subset you asked about:

| Permission | Org owner | Org manager | Team owner | Team member |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Setup SharePoint integration | ✓ | | | |
| Assign SharePoint site to team | ✓ | | | |
| Remove SharePoint site from team | ✓ | | | |
| View any connected SharePoint site | ✓ | | | |
| View connected SharePoint sites for team I'm a part of | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| Disconnect SharePoint integration | ✓ | | | |

**Recommended role setup for safe SharePoint access**

In Normain, every SharePoint *management* action — connecting the integration, assigning or removing sites from teams — is restricted to the **Org owner** role. No other role can connect or disconnect SharePoint sites.

1. **Keep Org owner tightly held** — a small number of trusted KPMG administrators. This role alone controls the SharePoint integration and every site-to-team connection.
2. **Distribute Org manager, Team owner and Team member freely** — none of them can connect, assign or remove SharePoint sites, so granting these roles does not widen SharePoint exposure.
3. **Mind the second lever — team membership.** A user reaches a site's files by being a member of a team connected to that site. Decide whether Team owners may manage their own team's membership, or whether that stays with Org owner / Org manager.

Net: SharePoint exposure is bounded by who holds **Org owner** and by team membership. Keeping Org owner with a few KPMG administrators keeps the surface minimal — and fits the one-site-per-engagement model we discussed.

Happy to walk through any of this or adjust the model to your governance requirements.

